            <ArticleTitle>Evaluating imaging-pathology concordance and discordance after ultrasound-guided breast biopsy.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.14366/usg.17049</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ultrasound (US)-guided breast biopsy has become the main method for diagnosing breast pathology, and it has a high diagnostic accuracy, approaching that of open surgical biopsy. However, methods for confirming adequate lesion retrieval after US-guided biopsy are relatively limited and false-negative results are unavoidable. Determining imaging-pathology concordance after US-guided biopsy is essential for validating the biopsy result and providing appropriate management. In this review article, we briefly present the results of US-guided breast biopsy; describe general aspects to consider when establishing imaging-pathology concordance; and review the various categories of imaging-pathology correlations and corresponding management strategies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
